HTML and XHTML are both markup languages used for creating web pages, but XHTML is a stricter,
more XML-based version of HTML. XHTML requires stricter syntax, including mandatory closing tags,
lowercase tags and attributes, and quoted attribute values. HTML is more lenient and allows for
optional closing tags and case sensitivity.

HTML (Hypertext Markup Language):
---------------------------------
Is the foundation of the web, defining the structure of web pages.
Has a more flexible syntax, allowing for optional closing tags and case sensitivity.
Is widely supported by browsers.
Continues to evolve, with versions like HTML5 introducing new features.

XHTML (Extensible Hypertext Markup Language):
---------------------------------------------
Is a stricter, XML-based version of HTML.
Requires all elements to be closed, with closing tags or trailing slashes.
Requires lowercase tags and attribute names, and quoted attribute values.
Uses XML parsing and validation tools.
Was intended to improve interoperability with XML and other technologies.
While still technically supported, it's less prevalent than HTML